1
00:00:00,950 --> 00:00:01,750
Every month,

2
00:00:01,750 --> 00:00:06,551
oil and gas operators tell public regulators exactly what they produced.

3
00:00:06,551 --> 00:00:08,619
The numbers are open to anyone.

4
00:00:08,619 --> 00:00:09,953
Almost no one looks.

5
00:00:10,516 --> 00:00:13,055
This is what happens when you put them on a globe.

6
00:00:16,016 --> 00:00:18,006
This is the Shell Asset Atlas.

7
00:00:18,006 --> 00:00:22,384
It's an unofficial portfolio project, with no connection to Shell,

8
00:00:22,384 --> 00:00:24,507
built entirely from public data.

9
00:00:25,016 --> 00:00:27,303
210 installations and sites.

10
00:00:27,303 --> 00:00:29,100
48 oil and gas fields.

11
00:00:29,100 --> 00:00:31,959
About 5,900 kilometres of pipeline.

12
00:00:31,959 --> 00:00:38,819
All of it drawn from regulators in the United States, the United Kingdom and Norway.

13
00:00:39,449 --> 00:00:40,776
Start in the US Gulf.

14
00:00:40,776 --> 00:00:42,799
Each column is a production hub,

15
00:00:42,799 --> 00:00:46,084
and its height is what that hub produced in a month.

16
00:00:46,550 --> 00:00:48,334
Point at one for a quick read.

17
00:00:48,334 --> 00:00:50,475
Click it, and the full record opens.

18
00:00:51,250 --> 00:00:52,501
This is Mars:

19
00:00:52,501 --> 00:00:58,951
a tension-leg platform, standing in 894 metres of water since 1996.

20
00:00:59,483 --> 00:01:04,557
Below that, every month of production since 2015, split into liquids and gas,

21
00:01:04,557 --> 00:01:07,654
with a link back to the regulator's own record.

22
00:01:09,316 --> 00:01:10,286
Now press play.

23
00:01:10,286 --> 00:01:13,325
A decade of production replays, month by month.

24
00:01:13,325 --> 00:01:17,657
The columns rise and fall, and the leaderboard re-ranks as it goes.

25
00:01:22,116 --> 00:01:22,814
Stop here:

26
00:01:22,814 --> 00:01:23,860
September 2021.

27
00:01:23,860 --> 00:01:26,789
Hurricane Ida struck at the end of August,

28
00:01:26,789 --> 00:01:32,577
and Shell-operated production in the Gulf fell by more than half in a single month.

29
00:01:32,577 --> 00:01:35,087
The Mars hub reports nothing at all.

30
00:01:35,716 --> 00:01:39,092
Press play again, and the recovery is there too.

31
00:01:39,092 --> 00:01:44,156
By December 2025, newer hubs like Vito and Whale are on the leaderboard,

32
00:01:44,156 --> 00:01:49,220
and the total is about a million barrels of oil equivalent a day, gross.

33
00:01:49,849 --> 00:01:50,854
Change the view.

34
00:01:50,854 --> 00:01:54,245
The seabed basemap shows why this is called deepwater:

35
00:01:54,245 --> 00:01:57,572
the platforms stand far beyond the continental shelf.

36
00:01:58,083 --> 00:02:01,879
Colour by water depth, then search for Perdido.

37
00:02:01,879 --> 00:02:04,867
It's moored in 2,388 metres of water.

38
00:02:06,716 --> 00:02:08,870
The leaderboard is a way in, too.

39
00:02:08,870 --> 00:02:11,807
Troll, off Norway, is the biggest name on it.

40
00:02:11,807 --> 00:02:13,895
But Shell doesn't operate Troll.

41
00:02:13,895 --> 00:02:15,527
It holds about 8 percent.

42
00:02:16,049 --> 00:02:18,690
So the atlas marks it as a partner asset,

43
00:02:18,690 --> 00:02:21,652
shows Shell's net figure beside the gross one,

44
00:02:21,652 --> 00:02:24,228
and draws the net share as its own line.

45
00:02:24,228 --> 00:02:26,353
Gross is never passed off as net.

46
00:02:27,483 --> 00:02:29,839
Shell is also a name on the street.

47
00:02:29,839 --> 00:02:35,696
OpenStreetMap volunteers have mapped more than 28 thousand Shell-branded fuel stations.

48
00:02:36,216 --> 00:02:40,620
One click turns them into a skyline, where taller means more stations.

49
00:02:40,620 --> 00:02:42,381
Then set the camera turning.

50
00:02:44,316 --> 00:02:45,809
Prefer to be shown around?

51
00:02:51,450 --> 00:02:53,384
The guided tour makes 12 stops,

52
00:02:53,384 --> 00:02:57,253
from the deepwater Gulf to a floating gas plant off Australia.

53
00:02:57,783 --> 00:03:01,047
Every figure in its text is computed from the data.

54
00:03:01,047 --> 00:03:04,503
So after each weekly refresh, the story is still true.

55
00:03:08,883 --> 00:03:11,276
And none of it has to be taken on trust.

56
00:03:11,276 --> 00:03:15,944
Data and method lists every source, its licence, and when it was last fetched.

57
00:03:16,616 --> 00:03:20,889
A pipeline rebuilds the whole atlas from those sources every week.

58
00:03:20,889 --> 00:03:26,067
If a regulator's site is down, it falls back to the last good copy, and says so.

59
00:03:30,616 --> 00:03:32,061
The Shell Asset Atlas.

60
00:03:32,061 --> 00:03:35,672
Public records, one globe, and a decade you can replay.

61
00:03:36,250 --> 00:03:40,176
It's an unofficial project, and it isn't endorsed by Shell.

62
00:03:40,176 --> 00:03:44,968
The data belongs to the regulators, and to OpenStreetMap's contributors.

63
00:03:44,968 --> 00:03:47,365
This narration is a synthetic voice.
